<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Korea and Japan</p><h1 class="st-title">Kuwana Domain</h1><p class="st-meta">1601 CE – 1871 CE · capital Kuwana</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A domain on the Tokaido road in Ise, guarding the sea crossing to Atsuta, whose last lord fought to the end for the shogunate.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sekigahara">Sekigahara</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuwana">Kuwana</a> to his famous general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honda-tadakatsu">Honda Tadakatsu</a> in 1601 with 100,000 koku. Tadakatsu rebuilt the castle and laid out the town at the mouth of the Ibi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiso-river">Kiso</a> rivers.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuwana">Kuwana</a> was the landing point of the Shichiri no watashi, the ferry across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ise-bay">Ise Bay</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokaido">Tokaido</a>, making it a busy post town. The domain was held by trusted Tokugawa <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> and related families, which were moved in and out; from 1823 it was ruled by the Hisamatsu-Matsudaira. Its last lord, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matsudaira-sadaaki">Matsudaira Sadaaki</a>, served as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> deputy alongside his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matsudaira-katamori">Matsudaira Katamori</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aizu">Aizu</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuwana">Kuwana</a> troops fought at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-toba-fushimi">Toba-Fushimi</a> in 1868, and Sadaaki continued the struggle in the north and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hakodate">Hakodate</a> while the domain surrendered. After abolition in 1871 it became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mie-prefecture">Mie Prefecture</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kuwana-domain.jpg" alt="Kuwana Domain" width="320" height="249"><figcaption>Image: Barfodt · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Matsudaira_Lords_of_Kuwana_in_Ise_Province_(A).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1601 CE – 1871 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kuwana</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Honda Tadakatsu, Matsudaira Sadaaki</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Japanese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Japan</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kuwana han</dd></dl></aside></div>
